<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; The Holy Spirit" cic="CCC 683-747" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">For use after class, in a group, or for personal study. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>Remembering the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>Who is the Holy Spirit? Is he a force, an energy, an influence — or a divine Person?</li>
<li>What does the name &laquo;Paraclete&raquo; mean, and why does Jesus use it to describe the Holy Spirit?</li>
<li>Name the seven gifts of the Holy Spirit as listed in Isaiah 11:2-3 and the Catholic tradition. For each one, write a single sentence explaining what it does in the soul.</li>
<li>What happened on the morning of Pentecost (Acts 2:1-4)? List the three signs — the sound, the sight, and the effect — and explain the significance of each.</li>
<li>According to Romans 8:26-27, what does the Holy Spirit do when we are unable to pray? Why is this one of the most comforting promises in all of Scripture?</li>
<li>What is the difference between the gifts of the Holy Spirit (given for personal sanctification) and charisms (given for the building up of the Church)? Give one example of each.</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question honestly and thoughtfully. There are no "correct" answers to these personal questions — only honest ones.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. Before reading this lesson, what image or idea did you have of the Holy Spirit? Was it a vague concept, a dove, a feeling, a force? How has your understanding changed now that you know he is a divine Person who dwells within you — with intelligence, will, and the capacity to love?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Take your time. Write freely. Sometimes the best way to discover what you really believe is to put it on paper.</p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
